so i am absolutely  the kind of person who would  take 15 minutes trying to decide  what ice cream flavor  it's called a paradox of choice  and it is an increasing problem  for a lot of people living in the modern  world  and it happens when neither of the  choices  are either good or bad and  you neither love or hate either of them  so your compass for making decision just  gets  all screwed up and when i look back at  that time well  actually i still do that it was like a  disease  i was plagued by the little decisions  that i had to make  and i didn't realize how much energy was  taking out of my life  until i read this article by  james clear on decision fatigue he talks  about this research by  the national academy of sciences where  they study  1100 judicial hearings over a 10-month  period so these were all  parole board judges who basically had to  decide  if the criminal had to stay in jail or  if they could be  released on parole and what they  realized is that  it wasn't actually the context or the  particulars of the crime that had the  biggest impact on their decisions  but it was actually the time of day so  at the beginning of the day  the percentage of a favorable ruling  would be up about  65 and near the end of the day it would  drop down to zero  and this happened all across the board  and so after reading this article  i decided to do a bit of an extreme  experiment and i basically decided i'm  gonna  stop making any small decisions  [Laughter]  so if i went to a restaurant i would  just ask the staff for recommendations  and if i got ice cream i would just tell  my friend to get me whatever she's  having  and then the second thing i started  doing is for any big decisions in my  life  i gave myself a time limit to make those  decisions  because i felt like yeah it's actually a  huge waste of my time  and i wasted my energy to keep thinking  about the same problem over and over  again  so i actually did this for six months i  ate whatever someone else  ordered for me for six months but what i  realized is that i  i actually felt a visible like a weight  lifted off of me and i just had more  energy to make  other decisions in my life so basically  willpower  is like it's like a muscle if you keep  using it throughout the day it's gonna  get tired and it's gonna fade away  throughout the day so here are some  slightly more practical ways  you can reduce decision fatigue and  increase  willpower so the first one take a couple  of minutes  the night before to make all the small  decisions  so plan out what you're gonna wear plan  out what you're gonna eat for breakfast  this way you're not wasting your  precious willpower on those  little decisions and number two do the  most important things  first you know studies have shown over  and over and over again that your mind  is more focused and clear in the morning  so you know whether it's like learning a  new language or writing a new book  try to do that right in the morning and  number three schedule your tasks  if you're let's say trying to lose  weight  schedule the workouts right in your  calendar if you  trust yourself to make that decision  after a long day of work you are  mistaken my friends  your willpower is going to run out and  it's gonna be harder  and harder for you to make those hard  decisions i  schedule my writing right in the morning  from 6 00 am to 7 30 am  i actually started doing this not long  ago and i  love it because i know that once i get  back from work  i'm tired all i'm gonna wanna do is just  chill on the couch and so yeah having  that right in my schedule it's a great  way to keep consistent  and then number four is eat  work out and recharge so  the study that i was talking about  before showed that  the judge's favorable rulings spiked up  right after they had their lunch breaks  you know obviously if you're eating  pizza  or junk food and you get into a food  coma after  that's not really gonna work out um so  try to eat healthy foods  and also sneaking in a little bit of  exercise during the day can also be a  really great way to recharge  for me i i bike to work  or i speed walk to work and  that actually helps me get really  energized for work in the morning  and if you want to try going the extreme  route that i did  and try cutting out all the small  decisions in your life  i would try it you'd be surprised at how  many of them  really doesn't make friends  you
 

